# Styling

The `styles.css` file is responsible for defining the visual appearance of the web application. It contains CSS rules that apply to various HTML elements, ensuring a consistent and appealing layout across the application.

## Global Styles

The universal selector `*` is used to reset the margin and padding for all elements to `0px`. This is a common practice in CSS to eliminate any default spacing that browsers may apply, allowing for a more controlled layout.

```css
* {
  margin: 0px;
  padding: 0px;
}
```

## Specific Element Styles

### Octocat Image

The `#octocat` ID selector styles an element with the ID `octocat`. It sets the display to `block`, which allows the element to take up the full width available, and centers it horizontally by applying `margin: 50px auto;`. The width is explicitly set to `384px`, ensuring that the image maintains a consistent size regardless of the viewport.

```css
#octocat {
  display: block;
  width: 384px;
  margin: 50px auto;
}
```

### Paragraphs

The `p` selector styles all paragraph elements. Similar to the `#octocat` styles, it sets the display to `block` and centers the paragraphs with `margin: 50px auto;`. The width is set to `400px`, and the font is defined to use a specific typeface stack, ensuring that the text is displayed in a clear and readable manner. The font size is set to `30px`, which makes the text prominent and easy to read.

```css
p {
  display: block;
  width: 400px;
  margin: 50px auto;
  font: 30px Monaco,"Courier New","DejaVu Sans Mono","Bitstream Vera Sans Mono",monospace;
}
```

Overall, the styles defined in `styles.css` enhance the user experience by providing a clean and organized layout, making the application visually appealing and easy to navigate.

